I wrote about this a bit before but it still blows my mind.
:has() let’s you have a single, canonical place for state in the DOM without the need to duplicate that state with JS in the form of classes just to achieve some styling effect. It’s so good
Quoting @jensimmons
Last, use a checkbox to toggle Dark Mode on &amp; off. No JavaScript. A checkbox input, placed anywhere in HTML is all I need, since I can change all my CSS across the DOM using :has()
body:has(input[type="checkbox"]:checked) {
background: blue;
--primary-color: white;
}
